September SurveyUSA poll showed only a 3-point Vasquez lead in an even-PVI district", "Trump's early-October endorsement of Cunningham and heavy attack-ad activity could nationalize the race"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point clearly to Vasquez: incumbency, a roughly 2-to-1 money advantage, a Likely D rating consensus, and markets pricing his win chances in the mid-90s. The lone recent poll showing a narrower 3-point race and the even partisan lean of the district argue for trimming the expected margin somewhat below the fundamentals-based estimate, but not enough to change the likely-D category.", "watch": "Whether post-endorsement polling shows Cunningham consolidating Republican support and closing within single digits, or outside spending surges on either side in the final weeks.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Gabe Vasquez (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent Gabe Vasquez seeks re-election in an even-lean southern New Mexico district against Republican Greg Cunningham. Vasquez holds a substantial fundraising advantage, with about $2.4 million on hand compared to Cunningham's roughly $794,000, and has picked up union endorsements while criticizing the administration's funding freeze. Cunningham gained attention with a 'complete and total' Trump endorsement in early October, and attack ads have flooded the district. Expert raters mostly call the race Likely Democratic, with a few at Lean Democratic and one at Safe Democratic. Prediction markets price Vasquez's win chances around 94 to 95 percent. Polling is sparse, but the one recent survey showed Vasquez ahead by three points among likely voters, suggesting a competitive but Democratic-favored contest.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8892, "margin": 7.53}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8688, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8708, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.87, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8306, "